# OCEANBOUND — MAJOR WORLD, PIRACY, SHIPBUILDING AND IMMERSION UPDATE

Update the existing Oceanbound game with all the features below.

**IMPORTANT: Do not remove, break or redesign existing gameplay.** Preserve the existing ocean physics, shipwrecks, gold, trading, survival, pirate factions, sea monsters, journal, music, ship customisation, crew management and simplified naval combat.

The goal is to create a living, detailed olden-times sailing world where the player can explore, trade, become a pirate, fight enormous fleets, encounter legendary sea monsters and gradually build an enormous, heavily armed ship.

# 1. GAME PACING AND BEGINNER EXPERIENCE

Make the overall game progression slightly slower and more natural.

The player should begin with a small wooden boat, 10 crew members, basic supplies and very limited resources.

Do not immediately overwhelm the player with dangerous pirate encounters.

During the early game:
- Pirates should be less aggressive towards the player.
- Large pirate fleets should be uncommon.
- The player should have time to learn the controls and explore.
- The first few encounters should mainly involve peaceful ships, small merchants and shipwrecks.
- Dangerous pirate attacks should become more common as the player explores further and builds their ship.
- Introduce more challenging enemies gradually.

**Do not make progression excessively fast.** Building a large ship, recruiting a substantial crew and acquiring powerful cannons should take time and effort.

### Tutorials and explanations

Explain new gameplay mechanics gradually.

For example:
- Explain sailing controls when the player first starts.
- Explain the inventory when they first collect resources.
- Explain trading when they first meet a merchant.
- Explain ship upgrades when they first open ship management.
- Explain cannons when they first purchase one.
- Explain reputation when they first interact with an innocent ship.
- Explain sea monsters when they first encounter one.

Use small, simple notifications and tutorial messages.

Do not display every tutorial at the beginning. Introduce features when they become relevant.

Allow the player to dismiss tutorials and disable repeated explanations.

# 2. LARGER TEXT AND USER INTERFACE

**Increase the size of all text and GUI elements throughout the entire game because the current interface is difficult to read.**

This applies to:
- Main HUD.
- Inventory.
- Ship management.
- Trading interfaces.
- Crew management.
- Journal.
- Map.
- Settings.
- Upgrade descriptions.
- Resource quantities.
- Notifications.
- Tutorial messages.
- Combat information.
- Reputation indicators.

Make text noticeably larger and easier to read.

Also slightly increase the size of buttons, tabs, icons and important interactive elements.

Do not simply enlarge everything until the interface becomes overcrowded. Adjust spacing and layouts to accommodate the larger text.

Make important information such as health, gold, provisions, water and ship condition particularly easy to read.

Add an optional UI scale setting so the player can increase or decrease interface size.

# 3. MORE SHIP TYPES AND A LIVING OCEAN

Add many types of historically inspired sailing vessels.

Include:
- Small fishing boats.
- Large fishing vessels and food trawlers.
- Small merchant boats.
- Medium merchant ships.
- Large merchant and cargo vessels.
- Passenger and travel ships.
- Supply and transport ships.
- Wealthy trading vessels.
- Small pirate boats.
- Medium pirate ships.
- Large pirate vessels.
- Armed naval and patrol ships.
- Rare treasure ships.
- Crown Fleet vessels.

Every ship should have a detailed 3D model, sails, hull design, crew, size, cargo and behaviour.

Ships should carry different quantities of gold, provisions, water and valuable cargo depending on their purpose.

Ships should independently travel along routes, encounter one another and react to nearby dangers.

# 4. MERCHANTS AND TRADING

Introduce different sizes and types of merchant ships with different inventories.

Not every merchant should sell the same items.

- Small merchants mainly sell basic timber, iron and cloth.
- Medium merchants carry larger quantities of basic materials and ship equipment.
- Large merchants carry substantial quantities of resources, weapons and specialised ship upgrades.
- Wealthy merchants occasionally sell rare construction materials, advanced equipment, dyes and flag designs.
- Specialised merchants should offer particular rare goods.

### Fishing trawlers

**Fishing trawlers must ONLY sell provisions and fresh water.**

They should carry different quantities of food and water, have varying prices and travel around the ocean like other ships.

They must not sell construction materials, cannons, weapons or other equipment.

### Trading

Allow the player to:
- Buy and sell resources.
- Select how many items to purchase.
- See individual prices and transaction totals.
- See the merchant's available stock.
- See their current gold balance.
- Purchase rare materials from specialised merchants.

Make sure transactions actually deduct gold and add or remove the correct inventory quantities.

Merchant prices and willingness to trade should be influenced by reputation.

# 5. PIRATE APPEARANCE AND SHIP DESIGNS

Pirate ships should use the same general sailing ship designs as ordinary ships.

**Do not make pirate ships look like completely different fantasy vessels.**

Use the same types of hulls and ship models that ordinary merchants, transport ships and other vessels use.

Make pirate ships recognisable through:
- Different sails and sail colours.
- Pirate flags and faction symbols.
- Different crew clothing and costumes.
- Different decorations.
- Distinctive colours and markings.

For example, a pirate merchant-sized vessel should still look like a normal merchant-sized sailing ship, but have different sails, a pirate flag and a crew dressed like pirates.

Different pirate factions should have their own sail designs, colours and crew outfits.

Large pirate ships should look like larger versions of existing sailing vessels rather than completely unrelated ship designs.

# 6. PIRATE FACTIONS AND RIVALRIES

Create several different pirate factions throughout the ocean.

Each faction should have its own:
- Name and flag.
- Sail colours and designs.
- Crew appearances and costumes.
- Personality and behaviour.
- Hunting areas.
- Relationships with other factions.
- Strength and number of ships.
- Leaders and important captains.

Some factions should be lifelong rivals and attack each other on sight. Others should occasionally form alliances, compete over treasure, fight over territory or betray one another.

Faction relationships should change throughout the player's journey.

Generate natural encounters involving ambushes, fleet battles, territory conflicts and temporary alliances.

The player should be able to intervene, support a faction or attack both sides.

# 7. PIRATE FLEETS AND CAPITAL SHIPS

Some pirate groups should travel together in organised fleets.

A fleet might contain:
- One enormous capital ship or flagship.
- Several medium pirate ships.
- Smaller, faster pirate boats.
- Armed escort vessels.

Not every group needs a capital ship.

### Pirate flagships

Create enormous sailing vessels with:
- Multiple decks.
- Several large masts.
- Numerous cannons.
- Large crews.
- Strong hulls.
- Large cargo capacity.
- Distinctive faction sails and flags.

Use the same general ship designs as other vessels, but make flagships substantially larger and more heavily equipped.

Other vessels should follow and protect their flagship.

Fleets should patrol, attack merchants, protect faction vessels, chase enemies, retreat when their flagship is badly damaged and fight rival fleets.

Defeating a flagship should be a major achievement with valuable potential rewards.

# 8. PIRATE RANKINGS AND BOSSES

Introduce pirate ranks:

1. Pirate Sailor.
2. Experienced Raider.
3. Pirate Officer.
4. Fleet Captain.
5. Admiral or Warlord.
6. Faction Leader.

Create special pirate bosses with:
- Unique names and appearances.
- Distinctive large sailing vessels.
- Large experienced crews.
- Powerful cannons.
- Unique faction flags.
- Different personalities and difficulty levels.
- Valuable potential rewards.

Some bosses should actively search for the player, while others remain with their fleets.

Defeating bosses should provide gold, resources, cargo and potentially captured ships.

Defeating faction leaders should weaken their factions and potentially allow rival factions to take over their territory.

Keep using the existing simplified naval combat system.

# 9. PIRATE SPAWNING AND DIFFICULTY

**Do not make all pirates progressively stronger as the player upgrades their ship.**

Even in the late game, smaller and weaker pirate ships must continue spawning throughout the ocean.

Maintain a mixture of:
- Small pirate boats with small crews and few or no cannons.
- Medium pirate vessels with moderate crews and weapons.
- Large pirate ships with experienced crews and several cannons.
- Powerful flagship-led fleets.
- Rare boss-controlled fleets.

Some pirates should be easy encounters even when the player has a massive ship.

Others should be extremely dangerous.

Use a varied spawning system so the player never reaches a point where every pirate encounter consists of enormous ships and overwhelming fleets.

# 10. PIRATE RELATIONSHIPS AND AUTONOMOUS ATTACKS

Pirates must operate independently throughout the world.

**Pirate ships and fleets must actively find, chase, attack and rob innocent NPC ships without the player's involvement.**

They should:
- Search for vulnerable ships.
- Pursue merchants and fishing boats.
- Demand gold and cargo.
- Attack ships that refuse to surrender.
- Steal resources and valuable cargo.
- Capture survivors.
- Retreat when badly damaged.
- Travel in groups.

Innocent ships should decide whether to surrender, escape or fight based on their size, crew, weapons, health and confidence.

NPC ships must fight each other using the existing simplified combat system.

The player should be able to watch, intervene, protect innocent ships, support pirates or ignore encounters.

# 11. PIRATE ALLIANCES AND TRADING WITH PIRATES

**If the player has a negative reputation for robbing innocent ships, allow them to become friendly with pirate factions and trade with them.**

The more notorious the player's reputation, the more likely certain pirate factions should be willing to interact with them.

Allow the player to:
- Approach friendly pirate ships.
- Trade gold for resources.
- Purchase basic construction materials.
- Purchase specialised materials.
- Purchase provisions and other supplies where available.
- Sell stolen cargo.
- Exchange information.
- Request assistance.
- Temporarily join pirate fleets.
- Help pirates attack rival factions.
- Form temporary alliances.

Different pirate factions should offer different goods and prices.

Some factions might specialise in weapons and iron, while others carry timber, cloth, stolen provisions or rare materials.

Some pirate factions should refuse to trade with peaceful players, while others may trade with anyone.

The player's relationships with individual factions should matter more than their overall reputation alone.

### Pirate trading interface

Use a trading interface similar to the existing merchant interface.

Display:
- Available pirate inventory.
- Item prices.
- Available stock.
- Player's gold.
- Player's resources.
- Buy and sell quantities.

Make all transactions functional.

# 12. THE CROWN FLEET — MAJOR NAVAL FACTION

Introduce a powerful new faction called **The Crown Fleet**.

The Crown Fleet is an organised naval force that patrols the ocean and protects peaceful ships from pirates.

They should feel like an established and powerful olden-times navy rather than ordinary merchants or pirates.

### Appearance and identity

Give The Crown Fleet:
- Recognisable and consistent flags.
- Distinctive royal colours.
- Matching crew uniforms.
- Unique ship decorations.
- Different ranks of naval officers.
- Several recognisable ship designs.

Their flags should make them immediately recognisable from a distance.

Their crew should wear matching naval uniforms, making them visually distinct from merchant sailors and pirate crews.

Their ships should use the same general historical sailing ship designs as other vessels, but with distinctive naval decorations, uniforms and equipment.

### Crown Fleet ship types

Include:
- Small patrol boats.
- Medium armed naval vessels.
- Large warships.
- Powerful fleet flagships.
- Ships carrying supplies and repair equipment.

Some Crown Fleet ships should patrol alone, while others should travel together in organised naval fleets.

### Behaviour towards pirates

The Crown Fleet should actively search for and attack pirate vessels.

They should:
- Patrol important sailing routes.
- Protect merchant ships.
- Respond to distress signals.
- Attack pirate ships.
- Fight pirate fleets.
- Attempt to capture or destroy pirate vessels.
- Protect innocent vessels from attacks.

They should occasionally engage pirate factions in large naval battles.

### Behaviour towards the player

The Crown Fleet's relationship with the player should depend on reputation.

**If the player has a good reputation:**
- Treat the player as a friendly captain.
- Protect the player's ship from pirates.
- Offer assistance.
- Share information about dangerous areas.
- Provide food and fresh water.
- Offer repairs using their own supplies.
- Occasionally escort the player's ship.

**If the player has a bad reputation for robbing innocent ships:**
- Become hostile.
- Recognise the player's vessel.
- Attempt to intercept the player.
- Demand surrender.
- Attack if the player refuses.
- Potentially send stronger naval fleets after the player if their reputation becomes extremely bad.

If the player has a neutral reputation, the Crown Fleet should generally remain cautious rather than automatically becoming hostile.

### Crown Fleet assistance

When a friendly Crown Fleet vessel approaches or the player approaches one, allow the player to request assistance.

They may offer:
- Provisions.
- Fresh water.
- Timber.
- Iron.
- Medical supplies.
- Emergency ship repairs.

They should visibly send crew members to help repair the player's vessel.

**Crown Fleet repairs must be an interaction the player can accept, not automatic repairs that happen without permission.**

They may provide basic emergency repairs for free or offer more extensive repairs depending on the player's reputation.

They should also assist nearby innocent ships that are damaged, low on provisions or under pirate attack.

### Crown Fleet and pirate faction interactions

The Crown Fleet should be hostile towards pirate factions and should occasionally attack pirate fleets independently.

Pirates should be able to attack Crown Fleet vessels as well.

Generate encounters where:
- Crown Fleet warships intercept pirate groups.
- Pirate fleets ambush naval patrols.
- Crown Fleet ships defend merchant convoys.
- Large pirate flagships fight against Crown Fleet warships.
- The player intervenes on either side.

Make The Crown Fleet a major part of the living ocean rather than simply another ship type.

